<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Mosque of Kairouan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mosque of Uqba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, also called the Mosque of Uqba, is the principal mosque of Kairouan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a> around 670. Its present form dates mainly from the Aghlabid rebuilding of 836 and later 9th-century additions, including a square minaret that is among the oldest standing in the Islamic world and a mihrab lined with lustre tiles. It is part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan.jpg" alt="Great Mosque of Kairouan" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MAREK SZAREJKO from CLONMEL, IRELAND - POLAND · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Mosque_of_Kairouan_Panorama_-_Grande_Mosqu%C3%A9e_de_Kairouan_Panorama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
